{"id": 24, "ts": 1779562384.6039915, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I\u2019d like to lay out the key evidence we have so far for each candidate against our core criteria:\n- **Candidate\u202fA** shows strong decision\u2011making (anticipates dangerous situations), technical insight (sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision) and leadership qualities. However, peers view A as a show\u2011off, not open to new ideas, which raises concerns for crew cooperation, professionalism and adaptability.\n- **Candidate\u202fB** is very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well, and assesses weather accurately \u2013 all vital for long\u2011haul ops. Technical competence is supported by strong computer skills. The main red flags are a weak numeric memory (attention/accuracy) and a tendency to make nasty remarks (professional communication/crew cooperation).\n- **Candidate\u202fC** makes quick, correct decisions, stays calm in crises and understands complex technology \u2013 strong on decision quality, stress resilience and technical competence. Yet C struggles to communicate ideas, is seen as egocentric and is not eager to pursue further education, which affect crew cooperation and adaptability.\n- **Candidate\u202fD** reacts adequately to unexpected events, concentrates well, solves problems exceptionally and takes responsibility seriously \u2013 covering stress resilience, attention, decision quality and responsibility. Concerns are a hot temper and being perceived as a know\u2011it\u2011all, which could impair crew cooperation and professional communication.\nGiven this overview, Candidate\u202fB appears to have the most balanced profile across reliability, stress resilience and technical competence, though we need more detail on the severity of the communication issues and the numeric\u2011memory gap.\n**Next step:** Could we get any additional observations from the interview notes on:\n1. Candidate\u202fA\u201